// Generated by tools/build_site.py — full lessons for one course, loaded on demand.
(window.SKILL_FULL = window.SKILL_FULL || {})["storytelling"] = [{"id": "the-three-part-story", "title": "The Three-Part Story", "track": "core", "minutes": 7, "summary": "Shape any work experience into a short situation–challenge–result story. You will learn to cut background, make the problem concrete and end with a clear outcome and lesson.", "sections": [{"heading": "Why a simple structure works", "body": "When you explain something at work, people listen for three things: where are we, what went wrong or what was at stake, and how did it end. If any of these is missing, listeners fill the gap with confusion or lose interest. A three-part story — situation, challenge, result — answers all three questions in the order the brain expects.\n\nThis structure is not only for speeches. You can use it in a team meeting update, a two-line WhatsApp message to your manager, a project review or an interview answer. The same idea scales from twenty seconds to five minutes.\n\nThink of it as a shape, not a script. The shape stays the same; the details change depending on who is listening and how much time you have.", "do": ["Use the same three parts for short updates and long talks"], "dont": ["Start telling a story before you know how it ends"]}, {"heading": "Situation: set the scene in one or two lines", "body": "The situation tells the listener who, where and when — just enough to follow the rest. Most people spend far too long here. \"So in March, our team, which has six people, actually seven if you count the intern, was working on a lot of things...\" loses the room before anything happens.\n\nA tight situation sounds like this: \"Last March, our Pune branch was onboarding about forty new customers a week, all on paper forms.\" One sentence, and the listener has the place, the time and the context.\n\nAsk yourself: what is the minimum the listener needs to understand the problem? Keep that, cut the rest. Names of systems, team history and side details can be added later if someone asks.", "do": ["Give who, where and when in one or two sentences"], "dont": ["Explain the full history of the team or project"]}, {"heading": "Challenge: make the problem concrete", "body": "The challenge is the heart of the story. It is the tension that makes people want to know what happened next. Vague challenges such as \"there were some issues\" create no tension at all.\n\nMake it concrete with a specific consequence: \"Forms were getting lost between the branch and the back office, and about one in ten customers had to come back to sign again.\" Now the listener can picture an annoyed customer making a second trip.\n\nIf you were personally involved, say what you decided or did — not only what \"we\" did. In a team story, it is fair to say \"The team agreed to try a checklist, and I drafted the first version.\" That is honest about the group effort and clear about your part.", "do": ["Name a specific consequence of the problem", "Say clearly what you decided or did"], "dont": ["Describe the problem only as \"some issues\" or \"challenges\""]}, {"heading": "Result: end with an outcome and a lesson", "body": "Stories that trail off — \"and then, yeah, it got better\" — waste the work done in the first two parts. End with what changed, ideally with a simple measure, and what you learnt.\n\nFor example: \"Within six weeks, repeat visits for signatures dropped from about one in ten customers to about one in fifty. The bigger lesson for me was to check the handover points first, because that is where things usually break.\"\n\nNot every story has a happy ending. A project that failed can still be a strong story if the result is honest and the lesson is real: \"We missed the deadline by two weeks. Since then I always build in a buffer for vendor approvals.\" Listeners trust an honest result more than a perfect one.\n\nA quick practice: pick one thing you did this month and write it in exactly three sentences — one for each part. Read it aloud and time it. If it takes more than thirty seconds, cut the situation first.", "do": ["End with a measurable change if you have one", "Share the lesson in one sentence"], "dont": ["Exaggerate the result to sound impressive", "Let the story fade out without an ending"]}], "takeaways": ["Every short story needs a situation, a challenge and a result.", "Keep the situation to one or two sentences.", "Make the challenge concrete with a real consequence.", "Be clear about your own role, even in team stories.", "End with an honest outcome and one lesson."], "quiz": [["Which opening best sets the situation for a short work story?", ["\"So basically, there were a lot of things happening at that time in our team.\"", "\"Last March, our Pune branch onboarded forty customers a week on paper.\"", "\"Let me first explain how our department was formed in 2015.\"", "\"You won't believe what happened.\""], 1, "It gives place, time and context in one sentence without unnecessary history."], ["Which part of the three-part story creates tension and makes people keep listening?", ["The situation", "The result", "The challenge", "The lesson"], 2, "The challenge is the problem or stake that makes listeners want to know what happened next."], ["Which challenge statement is strongest?", ["\"There were some issues with the process, and things were difficult for the team.\"", "\"Things were not ideal.\"", "\"We faced many challenges.\"", "\"About one in ten customers had to come back to sign again.\""], 3, "A concrete consequence lets the listener picture the problem."], ["Your team fixed a reporting delay, and you built the tracker. How should you describe your role?", ["\"The team agreed on a fix, and I built the tracker we used.\"", "\"I fixed everything myself.\"", "\"We did it\" — never mention yourself", "Skip your role completely and talk only about the final result instead"], 0, "It credits the team honestly while making your own contribution clear."], ["Your project missed its deadline. Can it still be a good story?", ["No — only success stories are worth telling in a professional setting", "Yes, if you hide the missed deadline", "Only if someone else was to blame", "Yes, if the result is honest and the lesson is real"], 3, "Honest outcomes with genuine lessons build trust with listeners."], ["A colleague's story takes two minutes and the problem appears only at the end. What should they cut first?", ["The result", "The lesson", "Background in the situation", "The challenge"], 2, "Over-long situations are the most common reason stories drag."], ["Which ending is weakest?", ["\"Repeat visits dropped from one in ten to one in fifty within six weeks.\"", "\"And then, yeah, it got better.\"", "\"We missed the date, so now I build in a buffer for approvals.\"", "\"The customer renewed for another year.\""], 1, "It gives no outcome and no lesson, so the earlier parts lose their value."], ["Where can the three-part structure be used?", ["Only in formal presentations in front of senior leaders", "Only in job interviews", "Only when telling success stories", "Updates, short messages, reviews and interviews"], 3, "The same shape scales from a two-line message to a five-minute talk."], ["You want to sound impressive and are tempted to round 6% up to \"almost half\". What is best?", ["Say \"about 6%\" and explain why it mattered", "Say \"almost half\" since nobody will check the exact figure", "Avoid numbers completely", "Say \"a huge improvement\" instead"], 0, "Exaggeration damages trust when it is discovered; an honest number with context is stronger."], ["You try telling your story in three sentences and it takes 50 seconds. What is the best first fix?", ["Speak faster", "Remove the result", "Shorten the situation sentence", "Add a fourth sentence to explain more"], 2, "The situation is usually where extra detail hides; trimming it keeps the challenge and result intact."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "The weekly update", "situation": "In your Monday team call, your manager asks each person for a one-minute update. Last week you found that a client's SIP mandate was failing because of a bank name mismatch, and you fixed it for that client and two others.", "options": [{"text": "\"Last week three SIPs were failing because the bank name on file didn't match. I found the mismatch, corrected all three, and now I check bank names during onboarding.\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Situation, challenge and result in three sentences, with a lesson that prevents repeat problems."}, {"text": "\"I worked on a lot of client issues, mostly mandates and some other stuff.\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "No concrete problem or outcome; the manager learns nothing useful."}, {"text": "Explain step by step how you investigated the mandate failure, including every call you made.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Too much process detail for a one-minute update; the result gets lost."}, {"text": "\"Fixed three failing SIP mandates — bank name mismatch.\"", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Clear and short, but it misses the lesson that could help the rest of the team avoid the same issue."}]}, {"title": "A failure you have to report", "situation": "You were asked to organise a client seminar in Nagpur. Only 12 people came against a target of 40, mainly because invitations went out just four days before. Your regional head wants to hear what happened.", "options": [{"text": "Focus on the venue and snacks, which went well, and mention attendance briefly at the end.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Hiding the main result damages trust, and the regional head will notice."}, {"text": "\"We targeted 40 and got 12. Invites went out only four days before, which was too late. For the next one, I'll send them three weeks ahead with a reminder two days before.\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "An honest result, a clear cause and a specific fix make a failure story credible and useful."}, {"text": "\"Attendance was low because people in Nagpur don't attend such events.\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "It blames the audience with a generalisation and ignores the real cause."}, {"text": "\"Attendance was 12 against 40. The invitations went late.\"", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Honest, but without a next step the story ends on the problem rather than a lesson."}]}, {"title": "Introducing yourself at an offsite", "situation": "At a company offsite, each new joiner gets about thirty seconds to share \"something interesting about yourself\". You once helped your college canteen move to UPI payments, which cut the lunch queue noticeably.", "options": [{"text": "List your hobbies, your hometown and your favourite films.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "A list is easy to forget; there is no story for people to remember you by."}, {"text": "\"In final year, our canteen queue took twenty minutes because everyone paid cash. I helped the owner set up UPI QR codes at both counters, and the queue dropped to about eight minutes.\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "A tiny story with a clear problem and a measurable result is memorable in thirty seconds."}, {"text": "\"I'm good at solving problems and making processes better.\"", "rating": "good", "feedback": "It's positive, but it's a claim without a story, so people are less likely to remember or believe it."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "book", "title": "Stories in three simple parts", "text": "Listeners want to know: where are we, what went wrong, and how did it end? Learn a three-part shape that works for a meeting update, a message or an interview."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "flag", "title": "Situation: one or two lines", "text": "Give who, where and when. Cut team history and side details.", "bad": "So in March our team, which had six people, actually seven with the intern, was doing a lot of things…", "good": "Last March, our Pune branch was onboarding forty customers a week on paper."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "target", "title": "Challenge: make it concrete", "text": "Tension keeps people listening. Name a real consequence.", "bad": "There were some issues with the process.", "good": "Forms got lost, and one in ten customers had to come back to sign again."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "user", "title": "Show your part honestly", "text": "In team stories, credit the team and say clearly what you did.", "example": "The team agreed to try a checklist, and I drafted the first version."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "trophy", "title": "Result: outcome plus lesson", "text": "Don't let the story fade out. End with what changed and what you learnt.", "bad": "And then, yeah, it got better.", "good": "In six weeks, repeat visits fell from one in ten to one in fifty. Lesson: check handover points first."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "shield", "title": "Failures make good stories too", "text": "An honest result with a real lesson earns more trust than a perfect ending.", "example": "We missed the deadline by two weeks. Now I always add a buffer for vendor approvals."}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "clock", "title": "Try it in three sentences", "text": "Pick one thing you did this month.", "items": ["Sentence 1: the situation", "Sentence 2: the challenge and your action", "Sentence 3: the result and lesson", "Read it aloud — under 30 seconds?"], "tip": "Too long? Trim the situation first."}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "star", "title": "You've got it!", "text": "Your three-part story in five lines:"}]}, {"id": "pitching-in-60-seconds", "title": "Pitching an Idea in 60 Seconds", "track": "core", "minutes": 7, "summary": "Pitch an idea to a manager, team or panel in about a minute. You will learn to lead with the problem, state your idea in one sentence, give one proof point and end with a clear ask.", "sections": [{"heading": "Start with the problem, not the idea", "body": "Most people open a pitch with their solution: \"I think we should build a WhatsApp chatbot.\" The listener's first thought is \"Why?\" — and they start looking for reasons to say no. If you start with a problem they already feel, they lean in instead.\n\nCompare: \"Our helpdesk gets around 300 calls a week, and nearly half are just people asking for their account statement.\" Now the listener is thinking about the problem with you. When you then share your idea, it sounds like an answer rather than an extra task.\n\nPick the problem that matters to this listener. A finance head cares about cost, a branch manager about customer complaints, a team lead about wasted hours. Same idea, different opening.", "do": ["Open with a problem the listener already cares about"], "dont": ["Open with \"I have an idea\" and the solution"]}, {"heading": "A simple 60-second shape", "body": "A one-minute pitch has room for about 130 to 150 spoken words. That is enough for five short parts: the problem (10–15 seconds), your idea in one sentence (10 seconds), how it works or one example (15 seconds), one proof point (10 seconds), and your ask (10 seconds).\n\nHere is the helpdesk pitch in full: \"Our helpdesk gets around 300 calls a week, and nearly half are people asking for their account statement. I'd like to let customers download statements by replying 'STATEMENT' on our WhatsApp number. It would send a password-protected PDF to the registered number only. A branch in our network tried this for a month and statement calls there fell by about 60%. Could I have two weeks and one developer to build a pilot for the Thane branch?\"\n\nRead it aloud and it takes under a minute. Every sentence earns its place.", "do": ["Write your pitch out and time it aloud", "Describe the idea in a single sentence"], "dont": ["Try to cover every feature and risk in one minute"]}, {"heading": "One proof point beats five claims", "body": "Listeners believe evidence more than enthusiasm. But in sixty seconds you have room for only one piece of proof, so pick the strongest: a small trial, a number from your own data, a customer quote or a comparable example.\n\nWeak: \"This will be very useful, customers will love it, it is easy and modern.\" Strong: \"When we tried it with 50 customers last month, 41 used it at least once.\" One fact like this does more than five adjectives.\n\nBe honest about the size of your proof. \"In a small trial\" or \"in one branch\" is fine — it signals you are realistic. Overclaiming (\"this will double our revenue\") invites tough questions you cannot answer and makes the rest of the pitch less believable.", "do": ["Use a small trial, a real number or a customer quote", "Say how big or small your evidence is"], "dont": ["Stack adjectives like \"amazing\" and \"game-changing\"", "Promise outcomes you cannot back up"]}, {"heading": "End with a clear, small ask", "body": "Many pitches end with \"So, what do you think?\" That hands all the work back to the listener. A good pitch ends with a specific, easy-to-approve request: time, budget, people or a next meeting.\n\nSmall asks get approved more often than big ones. \"Can I run a two-week pilot in one branch?\" is easier to say yes to than \"Can we roll this out across all branches?\" Once the pilot works, the next pitch is much stronger.\n\nAfter your ask, stop talking. Silence feels awkward, but filling it with extra points weakens the request. If the listener has questions, answer briefly and come back to the ask. Prepare two or three likely questions — cost, risk, who will do the work — so you are not caught off guard.\n\nPractice tip: record your pitch on your phone. Listen once for length and once for filler words like \"basically\" and \"actually\".", "do": ["Ask for something specific and small, like a pilot", "Pause after the ask and let the listener respond"], "dont": ["End with \"So, what do you think?\"", "Keep talking after you've made your ask"]}], "takeaways": ["Open with a problem your listener already feels.", "State your idea in one sentence.", "Use one honest proof point instead of many claims.", "End with a specific, small ask — then stop talking.", "Write it, time it and practise it aloud."], "quiz": [["Which opening is most likely to make a branch manager lean in?", ["\"I have a great idea for a new app.\"", "\"Technology is changing everything about how our customers bank these days.\"", "\"Nearly half our helpdesk calls are just for account statements.\"", "\"Can I take five minutes of your time?\""], 2, "It starts with a concrete problem the manager already feels."], ["Roughly how many spoken words fit in a 60-second pitch?", ["About 40", "About 130–150", "About 400", "About 600"], 1, "At a normal speaking pace, one minute holds about 130 to 150 words."], ["Which is the strongest proof point?", ["\"Everyone I spoke to said it sounds amazing.\"", "\"It is very modern and easy.\"", "\"It will surely double our branch revenue within the first year of launch.\"", "\"In a trial with 50 customers last month, 41 used it at least once.\""], 3, "A specific, honest result from a real trial is more believable than claims or predictions."], ["Which ending is best for a pitch to your manager?", ["\"Can I run a two-week pilot in Thane with one developer?\"", "\"So, what do you think?\"", "\"Anyway, that's my idea.\"", "\"Let me know whenever you are free to discuss this further.\""], 0, "It is a specific, small request that is easy to approve."], ["You are pitching the same idea to the finance head and then to a team lead. What should change?", ["Nothing; a good pitch should always be identical for every single listener", "The problem you open with, to match what each person cares about", "The idea itself, completely", "Only the length of your pauses"], 1, "The idea stays the same, but the opening problem should match each listener's priorities."], ["Your evidence comes from one branch over one month. How should you describe it?", ["As proof it will work everywhere", "Leave it out because it is small", "Say \"in a one-month trial at one branch\"", "Round it up to \"several branches\" to sound more convincing"], 2, "Stating the real size of your evidence shows you are realistic and keeps trust."], ["You make your ask and there is a few seconds of silence. What should you do?", ["Wait for the listener to respond", "Quickly add three more benefits", "Lower your ask immediately", "Apologise for taking their time"], 0, "Filling the silence weakens the request; give the listener time to think."], ["Why are small asks often better than big ones in a first pitch?", ["Managers dislike big ideas", "Small ideas never fail", "Big asks are against most company policies on budgets and approvals", "They are easier to approve and create proof for the next pitch"], 3, "A pilot lowers the risk for the approver and gives you evidence for a larger request later."], ["Which describes the idea in one clear sentence?", ["\"It's a kind of end-to-end digital transformation initiative for the whole customer journey.\"", "\"Customers reply 'STATEMENT' on WhatsApp and get a protected PDF of their statement.\"", "\"It's like an app, but also a bot, with some automation.\"", "\"It uses AI and cloud and will be very innovative.\""], 1, "It says exactly what the customer does and gets, without jargon."], ["You record your pitch and it runs 95 seconds. What is the best fix?", ["Speak much faster", "Remove the ask at the end to save time, since the idea speaks for itself", "Cut features and background; keep problem, idea, proof and ask", "Add a slide so you can talk less"], 2, "Trimming detail keeps the five essential parts while bringing the time down."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "The lift conversation", "situation": "You share a lift with your zonal head, Ms. Iyer, for about a minute. You have an idea: a short checklist that could reduce KYC rejections, which delayed 18 accounts in your branch last month.", "options": [{"text": "\"Ma'am, 18 accounts in our branch were delayed by KYC rejections last month. I've drafted a one-page checklist to catch errors before submission. Could I send it to you and try it in our branch for two weeks?\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Problem, idea and a small, specific ask — all in under a minute, and easy to say yes to."}, {"text": "\"Ma'am, I have a lot of ideas about improving processes. Can we meet sometime?\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Vague and asks for her time without giving a reason to give it."}, {"text": "Explain in detail every error type you found and how each checklist item addresses it.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Too much detail for a lift ride; she leaves without knowing what you want."}, {"text": "\"Ma'am, KYC rejections delayed 18 accounts last month. I've made a checklist that could help.\"", "rating": "good", "feedback": "A good problem and idea, but without an ask the conversation ends with nothing decided."}]}, {"title": "The overexcited pitch", "situation": "Your college team is pitching a campus tiffin delivery idea to a startup incubator panel. A teammate's draft says: \"This will be the next big unicorn and will capture the whole market in two years.\" You surveyed 120 hostel students and 74 said they would try it.", "options": [{"text": "Keep the unicorn line — panels like confidence.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Unsupported claims make experienced panels doubt everything else you say."}, {"text": "Replace it with: \"We surveyed 120 hostel students; 74 said they'd try it. We'd like support to run a one-month pilot in two hostels.\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Real evidence plus a small, testable ask is more credible than big predictions."}, {"text": "Drop all numbers and focus on how passionate the team is.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Passion helps, but without proof the panel has no reason to believe the idea works."}, {"text": "Say \"Most students we surveyed liked the idea.\"", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Honest, but vague; the actual numbers (74 of 120) are more convincing."}]}, {"title": "The question after your pitch", "situation": "You pitch a shared team calendar for client meetings to your manager. You end with, \"Can we try it for one month?\" She pauses, then asks, \"Who will keep it updated?\"", "options": [{"text": "\"I'll set it up and update it for the first month. If it saves time, we can rotate it after that.\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "A short, direct answer that removes the obstacle and brings her back to the ask."}, {"text": "Talk for two more minutes about all the other benefits of a shared calendar.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "It avoids her real question and buries your ask."}, {"text": "\"Everyone will update their own meetings.\"", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Reasonable, but she may doubt it will happen; owning the first month yourself is more reassuring."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "bolt", "title": "Pitch any idea in a minute", "text": "A minute is enough to win a yes — if every sentence earns its place. Learn a five-part shape for pitching to your manager, team or a panel."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "target", "title": "Lead with their problem", "text": "Start with your solution and they look for reasons to say no. Start with a problem they feel and they lean in.", "bad": "I think we should build a WhatsApp chatbot.", "good": "Nearly half our 300 weekly helpdesk calls are just for account statements."}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "clock", "title": "The 60-second shape", "text": "About 130–150 spoken words in all.", "items": ["Problem (10–15 sec)", "Your idea in one sentence", "How it works or one example", "One proof point", "Your ask"]}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "chart", "title": "One fact beats five adjectives", "text": "Pick your strongest evidence and say honestly how big it is.", "bad": "Customers will love it — it's easy, modern and game-changing!", "good": "In a trial with 50 customers last month, 41 used it at least once."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "flag", "title": "End with a small, clear ask", "text": "Don't hand the work back to the listener. Ask for something easy to approve.", "bad": "So, what do you think?", "good": "Could I have two weeks and one developer for a pilot in Thane?"}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "info", "title": "Then stop talking", "text": "Silence after your ask feels awkward. Let it be. Prepare short answers to likely questions — cost, risk and who will do the work.", "example": "\"I'll run it myself for the first month.\""}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "phone", "title": "Record and time it", "text": "Record your pitch on your phone. Listen once for length, once for fillers like \"basically\" and \"actually\". Over a minute? Cut features and background first."}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "Ready to pitch!", "text": "Your one-minute pitch, in short:"}]}, {"id": "stories-with-data", "title": "Telling Stories with Data", "track": "core", "minutes": 8, "summary": "Turn a page of numbers into a clear message people remember. You will learn to choose one key number, add context, use simple comparisons and check that every figure is correct.", "sections": [{"heading": "Find the one number that matters", "body": "When people present data, they often show everything they have: twelve columns, five charts and a percentage for every row. The audience does not know where to look, so they remember nothing. A data story starts by picking the one number that answers the listener's question.\n\nAsk yourself: if my manager remembers only one figure from this meeting, which should it be? For a branch review it might be \"complaints resolved within 48 hours\"; for a campaign it might be \"cost per new account\". Lead with that number, then use other figures only if they explain it.\n\nWrite your message as a sentence before you make any chart: \"Our average complaint resolution time fell from 6 days to 4 days this quarter.\" If you cannot write that sentence, you are not ready to present the data.", "do": ["Write your key message as one sentence first", "Lead with the number that answers the listener's question"], "dont": ["Show every column \"just in case\""]}, {"heading": "Context turns a number into meaning", "body": "A number on its own means very little. \"We opened 150 accounts\" — is that good? Context answers that question. The three most useful kinds are: compared with what (last month, target, another branch), out of how many, and over what time.\n\n\"We opened 150 accounts this month, against a target of 120 and 110 last month\" tells a clear story: above target and growing. \"We opened 150 accounts\" tells nothing.\n\nBe careful with percentages on small bases. If complaints went from 2 to 4, that is technically a 100% increase, but saying \"complaints doubled\" can alarm people unnecessarily. Give the actual numbers alongside the percentage: \"Complaints rose from 2 to 4 this month — small numbers, but we're checking the cause.\"", "do": ["Compare with target, last period or a similar team", "Show actual numbers alongside percentages"], "dont": ["Present a big percentage from a tiny base without the counts"]}, {"heading": "Make comparisons people can picture", "body": "Large or abstract numbers are hard to feel. Translating them into something familiar helps. \"We cut paper use by 60,000 sheets a year\" is fine; \"that's about 120 reams of paper we no longer buy\" is easier to picture, since a ream has 500 sheets.\n\nSimple ratios also work well. \"Resolution time fell from 6 days to 4 days\" can be said as \"a third faster\" — 2 days saved out of 6. \"Footfall rose from 120 to 150 visitors a day\" is \"a 25% increase\", because the extra 30 visitors is a quarter of 120. \"One in five customers\" is often clearer than \"20%\".\n\nWhatever comparison you choose, do the arithmetic yourself and check it twice. One wrong figure — saying \"50% faster\" when it was a third faster — makes people doubt every other number you show.", "do": ["Convert big numbers into familiar units", "Use \"one in five\" style ratios when they are clearer"], "dont": ["Use a comparison you haven't checked"]}, {"heading": "Keep charts simple and honest", "body": "If you use a chart, give it a title that states the message, not just the topic. \"Complaint resolution time fell from 6 to 4 days\" is a better title than \"Resolution time Q1–Q2\". The reader gets the point even if they glance for two seconds.\n\nChoose the simplest chart that works: a bar chart to compare categories, a line chart to show change over time. Highlight the one bar or line that matters and keep the rest in grey. Avoid 3D effects and pie charts with many slices.\n\nHonesty matters more than impact. A bar chart whose axis starts at 90 instead of 0 can make a small change look huge. Do not leave out the months that spoil the trend. If the data has a weakness — a small sample, a one-off event — say so. People forgive imperfect data; they do not forgive being misled.\n\nA quick exercise: take one table from your last report and write the single sentence it supports. Then decide which one number proves it.", "do": ["Use the chart title to state the message", "Highlight one bar or line and grey out the rest"], "dont": ["Start bar-chart axes above zero to exaggerate a change", "Drop inconvenient months from a trend"]}], "takeaways": ["Write your message as one sentence before building any chart.", "Lead with the one number that answers the listener's question.", "Add context: compared with what, out of how many, over what time.", "Use familiar comparisons, and double-check the arithmetic.", "Keep charts simple and never distort the data."], "quiz": [["Before making slides from a large data set, what should you do first?", ["Write the key message as one sentence", "Pick a colourful chart template", "Include every column so nothing is missed", "Calculate as many percentages as possible"], 0, "A one-sentence message tells you which numbers and charts you actually need."], ["Which statement gives the most useful context?", ["\"We opened 150 accounts.\"", "\"We opened a lot of accounts.\"", "\"We opened 150 accounts, against a target of 120 and 110 last month.\"", "\"Account opening was very good this month, much better than we expected.\""], 2, "Comparing with target and last month shows the number is both above target and growing."], ["Complaints went from 2 to 4 this month. What is the most responsible way to report it?", ["\"Complaints doubled!\"", "\"Complaints rose from 2 to 4 — small numbers, but we're checking why.\"", "Don't mention it at all, since such small numbers can't really matter to anyone", "\"Complaints increased by 100%.\""], 1, "Giving the actual counts prevents a big percentage on a tiny base from misleading people."], ["Resolution time fell from 6 days to 4 days. Which description is correct?", ["\"50% faster\"", "\"Twice as fast\"", "\"40% faster\"", "\"A third faster\""], 3, "Two days saved out of six is one third."], ["Footfall rose from 120 to 150 visitors a day. What is the percentage increase?", ["30%", "20%", "25%", "15%"], 2, "The increase is 30 visitors, and 30 is 25% of 120."], ["Which chart title is best?", ["\"Q1–Q2 data\"", "\"Complaint resolution time fell from 6 to 4 days\"", "\"Chart 3\"", "\"Resolution metrics overview for the first and second quarters\""], 1, "A title that states the message lets readers get the point at a glance."], ["You want to compare account openings across five branches. Which chart is most suitable?", ["A bar chart with your branch highlighted", "A 3D pie chart", "A table of 20 columns", "A line chart of five unrelated branches over one day"], 0, "Bar charts compare categories clearly, and highlighting directs attention."], ["A colleague's bar chart starts its axis at 90 so that a change from 92 to 95 looks dramatic. What's the problem?", ["Nothing — it makes the chart more exciting and easier to notice", "The bars should be 3D", "It should be a pie chart instead", "It exaggerates a small change and can mislead"], 3, "Truncated axes on bar charts distort how big a difference looks."], ["Which comparison makes 60,000 sheets of paper easier to picture?", ["\"About 120 reams of paper\"", "\"About 1,200 reams of paper\"", "\"About 12 reams of paper\"", "\"About 6,000 reams of paper\""], 0, "A ream has 500 sheets, so 60,000 sheets is 120 reams."], ["Your trend looks great except for two weak months. What should you do?", ["Remove those months from the chart", "Show all months and briefly explain the dip", "Change the chart type until the dip disappears", "Present only the best month"], 1, "Leaving out inconvenient data misleads; explaining it builds credibility."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "The monthly review deck", "situation": "Your manager asks you to present your branch's monthly numbers in five minutes. Your spreadsheet has 14 metrics. The biggest change is that new SIP registrations rose from 80 last month to 100 this month, against a target of 90.", "options": [{"text": "Show all 14 metrics on one slide so nothing is left out.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Too much data hides the key message; people remember nothing."}, {"text": "Lead with: \"SIP registrations rose from 80 to 100 — 25% up and above our target of 90,\" then show two or three supporting figures.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "One clear message with correct context and arithmetic, supported by only what explains it."}, {"text": "Say \"SIP registrations went up a lot this month\" and move on.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Without numbers or context, nobody knows how big the change was or whether it met the target."}, {"text": "Lead with \"SIP registrations rose from 80 to 100,\" then go through the remaining metrics one by one.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "A good start, but it misses the target comparison, and walking through every metric dilutes the message."}]}, {"title": "The tempting percentage", "situation": "You ran a small referral campaign: referrals rose from 3 last month to 9 this month. A colleague suggests the slide headline \"Referrals up 200%!\" for the regional meeting.", "options": [{"text": "Use \"Referrals up 200%!\" — it's technically correct.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Correct arithmetic, but a large percentage on a base of 3 gives a misleading impression of scale."}, {"text": "Write \"Referrals rose from 3 to 9 — early but encouraging; we'll track it for two more months.\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Honest counts plus context lets listeners judge the result themselves."}, {"text": "Write \"Referrals tripled this month.\"", "rating": "good", "feedback": "True and simpler, but still hides how small the base is; adding the counts would be better."}, {"text": "Leave the referral result out until it is bigger.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "An early positive signal is worth sharing if it is described honestly."}]}, {"title": "The spotted error", "situation": "Ten minutes before a client presentation, you notice your slide says \"processing time cut by 50%, from 10 days to 7 days\". The time did fall from 10 to 7 days.", "options": [{"text": "Leave it — no one will do the maths.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "If anyone does, every other number you present loses credibility."}, {"text": "Correct it to \"cut by 30%, from 10 days to 7 days\" before presenting.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Three days out of ten is 30%; fixing it takes a minute and protects your credibility."}, {"text": "Delete the percentage and keep only \"from 10 days to 7 days\".", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Accurate and safe, but the correct percentage would help listeners grasp the size of the change."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "chart", "title": "Make numbers tell a story", "text": "Twelve columns and five charts leave people remembering nothing. Learn to pick one number, give it context and present it honestly."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "target", "title": "Write the sentence first", "text": "If your manager remembers only one figure, which should it be? Write it as a sentence before building any chart.", "example": "Our complaint resolution time fell from 6 days to 4 days this quarter."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "search", "title": "Give every number context", "text": "Compared with what? Out of how many? Over what time?", "bad": "We opened 150 accounts.", "good": "We opened 150 accounts — target was 120, and last month we did 110."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "shield", "title": "Watch out for tiny bases", "text": "A big percentage on a small number can mislead. Show the counts.", "bad": "Complaints doubled!", "good": "Complaints rose from 2 to 4 — small numbers, but we're checking why."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "bulb", "title": "Use comparisons people can picture", "text": "6 days down to 4 is \"a third faster\". 120 to 150 visitors is \"25% more\". Always do the maths twice — one wrong figure spoils the rest.", "example": "60,000 sheets saved = 120 reams of paper."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "flag", "title": "Let the title say it", "text": "A reader who glances for two seconds should still get your point.", "bad": "Resolution time Q1–Q2", "good": "Complaint resolution time fell from 6 to 4 days"}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "check", "title": "Honest chart check", "text": "Tap each one before you share.", "items": ["Bar chart axis starts at zero", "No inconvenient months left out", "One bar or line highlighted, rest grey", "Weaknesses like small samples mentioned"]}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "Numbers, now with meaning!", "text": "Five habits for your next data story:"}]}, {"id": "storytelling-on-social-media", "title": "Storytelling on Social Media", "track": "core", "minutes": 7, "summary": "Write short posts on LinkedIn and similar platforms that people read to the end. You will learn to open with a strong first line, tell one small story honestly and protect other people's privacy.", "sections": [{"heading": "The first line decides everything", "body": "On LinkedIn, Instagram or X, people scroll fast. Most posts show only the first line or two before a \"see more\" link, so that opening has one job: make the right reader want to continue.\n\nA strong hook is specific and promises something. \"I'm happy to share that I completed a course\" is a statement many people make every day. \"I failed my first mock interview because I couldn't explain my own project\" is a moment — the reader wants to know what happened next.\n\nHooks can be a surprising result, a mistake, a question or a small scene: \"At 9:40 pm, the client called to cancel.\" Avoid tricks such as fake cliffhangers (\"You won't believe what my boss said...\") followed by an ordinary post. Readers notice, and it costs you trust.", "do": ["Open with a specific moment, result or mistake"], "dont": ["Use clickbait openings the post doesn't deliver on"]}, {"heading": "One post, one small story", "body": "The three-part story works well in posts: a quick situation, a challenge and what you learnt. Keep it to one idea. A post that covers your internship, your hobbies and your views on AI will be remembered for none of them.\n\nShort lines help on a phone. Write in two- or three-line paragraphs and leave a blank line between them. Lists can work for practical tips, but a post made only of bullets often loses the story.\n\nEnd with your lesson or a genuine question — \"How do you prepare for mock interviews?\" — not with a demand like \"Comment YES if you agree!\" Ask only if you plan to read and reply to the answers. Three to five relevant hashtags are plenty; a block of twenty looks like spam.", "do": ["Keep one idea per post", "Use short paragraphs with space between them"], "dont": ["Pack several unrelated stories into one post", "End with engagement bait like \"Comment YES\""]}, {"heading": "Stay honest about what happened", "body": "Social media rewards dramatic stories, which creates pressure to exaggerate. A story where you \"almost gave up\" or where a stranger's single sentence \"changed your life\" may get likes, but colleagues and recruiters who know the real facts will notice.\n\nStick to what actually happened. If a result was a team effort, say so and tag the team (with their permission). If you are sharing numbers — \"grew my page to 5,000 followers\" or \"cleared the exam with 92 percentile\" — make sure they are accurate and not rounded up.\n\nBe careful with claims about money, investments or careers. Saying \"this one habit made me rich\" or presenting one person's outcome as typical can mislead readers who make real decisions based on it. Share your experience as your experience: \"This worked for me; it may not suit everyone.\"", "do": ["Credit the team for team results", "Share your experience as one person's experience"], "dont": ["Add drama or numbers that didn't happen", "Present one outcome as what everyone can expect"]}, {"heading": "Protect other people's privacy", "body": "The best stories often involve other people — a customer, a manager, a candidate you interviewed. Their privacy matters more than your post. Before sharing, ask: would this person be comfortable if they read this and recognised themselves?\n\nNever post a client's name, account details, screenshots of chats or emails, or anything your employer treats as confidential. For finance professionals this is especially important; client information must stay private. Change or remove identifying details: \"a retired teacher I met last year\" rather than \"Mrs. Kulkarni from our Dadar branch\".\n\nIf you want to tell a story about a colleague or a specific moment at work, ask first. Check your company's social media policy too — many firms have rules about what employees can say about clients, results or internal matters.\n\nQuick exercise: draft a post about something you learnt this month. Read it once as the person most involved in the story. If anything would embarrass them, change it.", "do": ["Remove names and identifying details", "Ask before posting about a colleague", "Check your company's social media policy"], "dont": ["Share screenshots of client chats or emails", "Post confidential results or numbers"]}], "takeaways": ["Write a specific first line that earns the \"see more\" click.", "Tell one small story per post in short paragraphs.", "Keep stories and numbers honest; credit the team.", "Never share client details, screenshots or confidential information.", "Ask before posting about others, and follow your company's policy."], "quiz": [["Which first line is most likely to make the right reader click \"see more\"?", ["\"I'm happy to share that I completed a course.\"", "\"Hello connections! Hope everyone is doing well and having a really productive week.\"", "\"I failed my first mock interview because I couldn't explain my own project.\"", "\"You won't believe this!!!\""], 2, "A specific moment creates curiosity and promises a real story."], ["A post opens with \"You won't believe what my boss said...\" and then shares an ordinary update. What's the problem?", ["It's too short", "The hook makes a promise the post doesn't keep, which costs trust", "It doesn't use enough hashtags", "It should have been posted in the morning, when more people are scrolling"], 1, "Clickbait that doesn't deliver makes readers less likely to trust your next post."], ["How many ideas should a typical storytelling post focus on?", ["One", "Three", "Five", "As many as fit in the character limit"], 0, "One idea per post makes it easier to read and remember."], ["Which ending is best for a post about preparing for interviews?", ["\"Comment YES if you agree, and tag three friends who need this!\"", "\"Like and share for more!\"", "Twenty hashtags in a block", "A genuine question — and you reply to the answers"], 3, "A genuine question you follow up on invites real conversation, unlike engagement bait."], ["You led part of a team project that won an internal award. How should your post describe it?", ["\"I won an award for my project.\"", "Credit the team, tag them with permission, and explain your part", "Don't mention the team to keep it short", "Say the project was your idea from the start, since you led part of it"], 1, "Honest credit protects your reputation with colleagues who know the facts."], ["You want to share a story about a client who was nervous about investing. What should you do?", ["Use her name so the story feels real", "Post a screenshot of her WhatsApp message", "Mention her branch and city so that readers get the full context", "Remove identifying details and check your company's policy"], 3, "Client information must stay private; identifying details and screenshots should never be posted."], ["Which statement about your personal investing experience is most responsible?", ["\"This one habit made me rich — it'll work for you too.\"", "\"Anyone can get these returns.\"", "\"This worked for me; it may not suit everyone.\"", "\"Follow my exact portfolio for guaranteed results.\""], 2, "It shares experience without implying that one outcome is typical or assured."], ["What is the best paragraph style for a post read on a phone?", ["Short paragraphs of two or three lines with space between", "One long block of text", "Only bullet points", "All capital letters for the key lines, so nobody misses them"], 0, "Short, spaced paragraphs are easier to read on a small screen."], ["Before posting a story involving a colleague, what is the best step?", ["Post it and simply remove it later if they complain about it", "Tag them without asking so they see it", "Change only their first name", "Ask them first if they are comfortable with it"], 3, "Asking first respects their privacy and avoids awkwardness later."], ["You cleared an exam at the 91.4 percentile. Your friend says to post \"95+ percentile\". What should you do?", ["Post 95+ since it's close enough", "Post the actual figure, or leave the number out", "Post 99 percentile for more impact", "Say \"top 1%\""], 1, "Rounding up results is dishonest and can be checked by recruiters."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "A great client moment", "situation": "A first-time investor, a 58-year-old shopkeeper in Indore, thanked you warmly after you patiently explained how SIPs work. You'd like to post about what this taught you about explaining things simply.", "options": [{"text": "Post a photo with him and his name, with his message screenshot.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Sharing a client's identity and private messages breaks confidentiality, even with good intentions."}, {"text": "Write about the lesson — \"explain once with a simple example, then ask what's unclear\" — and describe him only as \"a first-time investor\", after checking your firm's policy.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "The lesson is shared, the client cannot be identified, and you have followed company rules."}, {"text": "Describe him as \"a shopkeeper from Indore in his late fifties\" without his name.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Better, but these details could still identify him to people who know him; keep it more general."}, {"text": "Don't post anything — client stories are never allowed.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Too cautious; an anonymised lesson that follows policy can be shared responsibly."}]}, {"title": "Making it more dramatic", "situation": "You got a job offer after three months of applications. A friend reviewing your draft post says, \"Write that you were rejected 100 times and almost gave up — it'll go viral.\" You actually got about 25 rejections and never thought of giving up.", "options": [{"text": "Use the bigger number — viral posts need drama.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "False numbers can be noticed by people who know you and damage trust permanently."}, {"text": "Write the real story: about 25 rejections, and the one change that made the difference.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "An honest, specific story with a useful lesson is valuable to readers and safe for your reputation."}, {"text": "Skip the rejections and just announce the offer.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Honest, but you lose the useful part of the story that could help other job seekers."}, {"text": "Say \"countless rejections\" to avoid giving a number.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Vague exaggeration is still exaggeration."}]}, {"title": "Team results on your feed", "situation": "Your team's new onboarding process cut average account-opening time at your branch from 5 days to 3 days. You want to post about it, and your company has a social media policy.", "options": [{"text": "Post the numbers immediately with your branch name — it's good news.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Even positive internal results may be confidential; check the policy first."}, {"text": "Check the policy, ask your manager whether the result can be shared, then post crediting the team.", "rating": "best", "feedback": "It respects company rules and gives fair credit to everyone involved."}, {"text": "Post about the general lesson — \"fixing handovers saves days\" — without numbers or branch name.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Safe, but asking first might let you share the full story with proper credit to the team."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "phone", "title": "Posts people read to the end", "text": "Most posts show only one or two lines before \"see more\". Learn to write a strong hook, tell one honest story and protect other people's privacy."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "bolt", "title": "Hook with a real moment", "text": "A specific moment, result or mistake makes readers want more.", "bad": "I'm happy to share that I completed a course.", "good": "I failed my first mock interview because I couldn't explain my own project."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "grid", "title": "One post, one story", "text": "Use situation, challenge and lesson. Write two- or three-line paragraphs with space between. Three to five hashtags are plenty."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "user", "title": "End with a real question", "text": "Ask only if you'll read and reply to the answers.", "bad": "Comment YES if you agree!", "good": "How do you prepare for mock interviews?"}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "star", "title": "Keep it honest", "text": "Colleagues and recruiters know the real facts. Never round up results or add drama.", "bad": "Rejected 100 times, almost gave up — then everything changed!", "good": "About 25 rejections. Here's the one change that made the difference."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "shield", "title": "Protect other people's privacy", "text": "Never post client names, account details or chat screenshots.", "bad": "Mrs. Kulkarni from our Dadar branch messaged me today…", "good": "A retired teacher I met last year taught me something."}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "check", "title": "Before you post", "text": "Tap through each one.", "items": ["First line is specific, not clickbait", "Numbers are exact, not rounded up", "No one can be identified without consent", "Company social media policy checked"], "tip": "Reread it as the person in the story."}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "Ready to post!", "text": "Five rules for your next post:"}]}, {"id": "stories-in-interviews", "title": "Using Stories to Stand Out in Interviews and GDs", "track": "fresher", "minutes": 8, "summary": "Answer interview questions with short, specific stories instead of claims. You will learn to build a small story bank, fit stories to common questions and use examples briefly in group discussions.", "sections": [{"heading": "Why claims don't work but stories do", "body": "Almost every candidate in a campus placement says \"I'm hardworking, a team player and a quick learner.\" Interviewers hear these words dozens of times a day, and they can't check any of them. A short story is different: it gives evidence, and it is the thing the panel remembers when comparing candidates later.\n\nCompare: \"I'm good under pressure\" with \"In my final-year project, our data collection failed two weeks before submission. I redesigned the survey, our group collected 200 responses in five days, and we submitted on time.\" The second answer proves the claim without repeating it.\n\nYou don't need dramatic achievements. Freshers can draw from college projects, internships, part-time work, family businesses, sports, volunteering and even exam preparation.", "do": ["Back each strength with a real example"], "dont": ["List adjectives about yourself without proof"]}, {"heading": "Build a story bank of five or six stories", "body": "Before interviews, prepare five or six short stories that cover common themes: a challenge you solved, a time you worked in a team, a time you led something, a mistake or failure, a time you learnt something quickly, and a disagreement you handled.\n\nWrite each one in three parts: situation, challenge and your action, and result with a lesson. Keep each to about 45–60 seconds when spoken. One good story can answer several questions — a college fest you organised might show leadership, teamwork and handling pressure, depending on which part you emphasise.\n\nIf you prepared for banking, SSC or other competitive exams, that experience counts. \"I prepared for bank PO exams for a year while tutoring two students for income. I learnt to plan my week in blocks and track my mock scores every Sunday\" shows discipline and planning.", "do": ["Prepare stories for challenge, teamwork, leadership, failure and learning", "Practise each to under a minute"], "dont": ["Memorise word-for-word scripts that sound rehearsed"]}, {"heading": "Fit the story to the question", "body": "A common mistake is telling a good story that doesn't answer the question asked. If the interviewer asks about a disagreement, your story must include a real difference of opinion and how you handled it — not just a project that went well.\n\nStart your answer with a one-line summary that links to the question: \"Sure — the clearest example is when my project partner and I disagreed about our survey method.\" Then tell the story. End by connecting it back: \"Since then, I try to agree on the method before we split the work.\"\n\nFor \"Tell me about a weakness\" or \"a failure\", choose a real one and spend most of the time on what you changed. Interviewers are not looking for a perfect candidate; they want to see honesty and self-awareness. Never invent a story — follow-up questions quickly reveal it.", "do": ["Open with a one-line link to the question", "End by connecting the lesson back"], "dont": ["Force your favourite story onto every question", "Invent or exaggerate a story"]}, {"heading": "Short examples in group discussions", "body": "Group discussions reward people who move the conversation forward, not those who talk longest. A brief, relevant example is one of the best ways to do this. On a topic like \"Should UPI transactions be charged?\", a quick example — \"My uncle's kirana store in Satara now takes almost all payments through UPI; a small charge might push him back to cash\" — makes an abstract point concrete.\n\nKeep GD examples to two or three sentences. Use them to support a point, then hand the discussion back: \"That's one side — does anyone see it differently?\" This shows you can listen as well as speak.\n\nAvoid made-up statistics. If you are not sure of a figure, say \"I've read that UPI handles most small digital payments\" rather than inventing a precise number. A panel member may know the real figure.\n\nExercise: write your story bank on one page, with a three-word label for each story. Before any interview, read the page once.", "do": ["Use a two- or three-sentence example to support a point", "Invite others in after your point"], "dont": ["Tell long personal stories in a GD", "Quote statistics you're not sure of"]}], "takeaways": ["Prove strengths with short stories, not adjectives.", "Prepare a bank of five or six stories covering common themes.", "Link each story clearly to the question asked.", "Pick real failures and focus on what you changed.", "In GDs, use brief examples and invite others in."], "quiz": [["An interviewer asks, \"Are you good under pressure?\" Which answer is strongest?", ["\"Yes, I'm very good under pressure.\"", "\"Pressure brings out the best in me.\"", "\"I think so — most of my friends and professors have always told me that I handle pressure really well.\"", "\"When our survey failed two weeks before submission, I redesigned it and we submitted on time.\""], 3, "A specific story proves the claim instead of just repeating it."], ["How many stories should a typical story bank have?", ["One really good one", "Five or six covering common themes", "At least twenty", "None — answers should be spontaneous"], 1, "Five or six stories can cover most behavioural questions, and each can be adapted."], ["You are asked about a disagreement. Your best story is about a project that went smoothly. What should you do?", ["Tell it anyway — it's your best story", "Say you've never disagreed with anyone, so you seem easy to work with", "Choose a story that includes a real difference of opinion", "Change the subject to your strengths"], 2, "A story must answer the question asked; otherwise it seems you missed the point."], ["Which is the best way to begin a story answer?", ["A one-line summary linking it to the question", "Your full academic background", "\"Let me think...\" followed by a long pause", "A joke to break the ice"], 0, "A clear opening tells the interviewer how the story will answer their question."], ["What should most of a \"Tell me about a failure\" answer focus on?", ["How others caused the failure", "Why the failure wasn't really your fault", "What you changed afterwards", "How rare your failures are"], 2, "Interviewers look for honesty and learning, not blame or perfection."], ["You prepared for bank exams for a year before campus placements. Can it be an interview story?", ["No, it's unrelated to work", "Yes, it can show discipline and planning with specific habits", "Only if you cleared the exam", "Only in government job interviews, where exam preparation is relevant"], 1, "Exam preparation can show real skills like planning, consistency and tracking progress."], ["How long should a story in a group discussion usually be?", ["Two or three sentences", "About two minutes", "As long as needed to convince everyone", "Five minutes, to take control"], 0, "Short examples support your point and leave time for others to contribute."], ["In a GD, you aren't sure of an exact statistic. What should you do?", ["Guess a precise number confidently", "Say the number is from a government report so nobody questions it", "Skip the point completely", "Make the point without inventing a precise figure"], 3, "Invented figures can be challenged; general wording keeps your point honest."], ["Why is memorising stories word for word risky?", ["Interviewers don't allow prepared answers", "Stories need to be at least five minutes long to sound properly detailed", "It can sound rehearsed and break down under follow-up questions", "Memorised stories are always too short"], 2, "Knowing the three parts well lets you speak naturally and adapt."], ["After making your point with an example in a GD, what is a good next move?", ["Repeat your point louder", "Invite others: \"Does anyone see it differently?\"", "Interrupt the next speaker to add more points before anyone else can", "Stay silent for the rest of the GD"], 1, "Bringing others in shows you can listen and lead the discussion."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "The teamwork question", "situation": "In a campus interview for a management trainee role at a bank, the panel asks, \"Tell us about a time you worked in a team.\" You organised a blood donation camp with four classmates, and you handled getting permissions from the college and the hospital.", "options": [{"text": "\"I'm a strong team player and always cooperate with everyone.\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "A claim with no evidence; the panel learns nothing new."}, {"text": "\"Our five-member team ran a blood donation camp. I handled permissions from the college and hospital, which were delayed twice; I set up a joint call to clear them. We collected 140 units, and I learnt to sort out approvals early.\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Clear team context, your specific role, a challenge, a result and a lesson — all in under a minute."}, {"text": "Describe the whole camp from planning to cleanup, including every team member's role.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "It shows teamwork, but the panel may lose track of what you did; focus on your part and the result."}, {"text": "\"I prefer working alone, but I can work in teams when needed.\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "It doesn't answer the question and raises doubts about your fit."}]}, {"title": "The weakness question", "situation": "A panel asks, \"What is your biggest weakness?\" Honestly, you used to take on too many tasks and miss small deadlines, and you now use a simple daily list to manage this.", "options": [{"text": "\"I'm a perfectionist and work too hard.\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "A common disguised strength; panels see through it and it suggests a lack of self-awareness."}, {"text": "\"I used to say yes to everything and miss small deadlines. During my internship I started a daily three-item priority list, and I haven't missed a deadline since.\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "A real weakness, a specific fix and evidence of change — honest and reassuring."}, {"text": "\"I sometimes take on too much, but I'm working on it.\"", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Honest, but without a specific action or result, it doesn't show real progress."}]}, {"title": "The GD on work from home", "situation": "In a group discussion on \"Should companies continue work from home?\", two candidates are repeating general points loudly. You remember your cousin, an analyst in Bengaluru, saving about three hours a day in commute time.", "options": [{"text": "Wait for a pause and say: \"One concrete angle — my cousin in Bengaluru saves about three hours of commuting daily on WFH days. But that's one case; how do teams keep collaboration strong?\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "A brief, honest example that adds something new, then a question that moves the group forward."}, {"text": "Interrupt and tell the full story of your cousin's job and daily routine.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Interrupting and a long story make you seem unable to listen or stay brief."}, {"text": "Make the point that commuting wastes time, without the example.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "A fair point, but the concrete example would make it more memorable and credible."}, {"text": "Say \"Studies show WFH increases productivity by 47%\" to sound authoritative.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "An invented statistic can be challenged and damages your credibility."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "briefcase", "title": "Stand out with stories", "text": "Every candidate says \"hardworking team player\". A one-minute story proves it — and it's what the panel remembers later. Learn to build and use your story bank."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "target", "title": "Prove it, don't claim it", "text": "Panels can't check adjectives. A short story gives them evidence.", "bad": "I'm very good under pressure.", "good": "Our survey failed two weeks before submission. I redesigned it, we got 200 responses in five days and submitted on time."}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "book", "title": "Build your story bank", "text": "Prepare one short story for each. Keep each under a minute.", "items": ["A challenge you solved", "A team you worked in", "Something you led", "A mistake and what you changed", "A disagreement you handled"], "tip": "College projects, exam prep and family businesses count."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "flag", "title": "Link the story to the question", "text": "Open with one line that connects to what was asked. End by tying the lesson back.", "example": "\"The clearest example is when my project partner and I disagreed about our survey method.\""}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "refresh", "title": "Real weaknesses, real fixes", "text": "Panels want honesty and self-awareness, not perfection.", "bad": "I'm a perfectionist and work too hard.", "good": "I used to miss small deadlines. Now I keep a daily three-item priority list."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "user", "title": "GD examples: short, then share", "text": "Two or three sentences, then bring others in.", "bad": "Studies show UPI charges would cut usage by 63%! (made-up figure)", "good": "My uncle's kirana store takes almost all payments by UPI. Does anyone see it differently?"}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "shield", "title": "Never invent a story", "text": "Follow-up questions quickly reveal made-up stories. Know your three parts well rather than memorising a script word for word."}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "Interview-ready!", "text": "Your story toolkit for interviews and GDs:"}]}, {"id": "stories-that-help-clients-decide", "title": "Stories That Help Clients Understand Investing", "track": "professional", "minutes": 9, "summary": "Use clearly labelled illustrative stories to explain goals, time horizons and risk to clients. You will learn to make concepts relatable without cherry-picking returns, implying guarantees or skipping suitability and cost disclosure.", "sections": [{"heading": "Why stories help clients understand", "body": "Many clients find terms like \"volatility\", \"asset allocation\" or \"time horizon\" hard to connect with their lives. A short story about someone with a similar goal can make the idea click: \"Imagine a couple saving for their daughter's college fees in twelve years...\" The client starts thinking about their own situation, not about jargon.\n\nBut stories are powerful, which is exactly why they must be used carefully. A story about one investor who made a lot of money can create unrealistic expectations faster than any brochure. The goal of a client story is understanding, not persuasion to buy a product.\n\nThe principles in this lesson follow the spirit of the conduct expectations for distributors and advisers: put suitability first, never promise or imply returns, disclose costs and commission, and avoid anything that could mislead.", "do": ["Use stories to explain concepts, not to push a product"], "dont": ["Use a story to create urgency or excitement about returns"]}, {"heading": "Label illustrations clearly", "body": "Every example you share should be clearly marked as an illustration, not a real client's result or a forecast. Simple phrases work: \"This is just an illustration to explain the idea\" or \"These are made-up numbers to show how it works, not what any fund will return.\"\n\nUse composite or imaginary characters rather than real clients. \"Take an imaginary investor, say Meera, who is 35...\" protects real clients' privacy and makes it clear you are explaining a concept.\n\nWhere you use numbers, keep them simple and focus on what the client controls. For example: \"If Meera invests ₹5,000 a month for ten years, she will have put in ₹6 lakh of her own money. What it grows to depends on the market — it could be more or less, and in some years the value may fall below what she invested.\" The arithmetic is correct, and no return is promised.", "do": ["Say \"This is an illustration\" every time", "Use imaginary characters instead of real clients"], "dont": ["Present a past return as what the client can expect", "Share a real client's story or portfolio"]}, {"heading": "Stories about goals and time", "body": "The most useful client stories connect investments to goals and timelines. A story about two goals can explain why different products suit different needs: \"Imagine Arjun needs ₹2 lakh for a wedding in eighteen months, and also wants to build a retirement fund over twenty-five years. Money needed soon usually shouldn't be in something that can fall sharply just before he needs it. Money for twenty-five years away can generally handle more ups and downs.\"\n\nThis kind of story helps the client think about their own goals before you discuss any product. It naturally leads into a suitability conversation: \"What are you saving for, and when will you need it?\"\n\nAvoid stories that compare a client with a richer friend or neighbour (\"Your colleague already has ₹50 lakh in equity\"). Comparison creates pressure, not understanding, and that colleague's risk profile is not your client's.", "do": ["Link stories to the client's own goals and timelines", "Use the story to start a suitability conversation"], "dont": ["Compare the client with friends or neighbours to create pressure"]}, {"heading": "Stories about risk — told honestly", "body": "Risk stories matter most, because clients who don't expect falls are the ones who panic and sell at the worst time. Explain falls before they happen: \"Imagine your ₹10 lakh portfolio shows ₹8 lakh after a bad few months — a 20% fall. How would you feel? What would you do?\" This is an illustration, but it helps you judge whether the client's risk appetite matches the product.\n\nAvoid cherry-picking. Showing only the best five-year period of a fund, or the story of one investor who \"made 3x in five years\", gives a false picture. If you show past performance, use the full, standard figures from official documents, include the weak periods, and say clearly that past performance does not indicate future returns.\n\nFinally, be open about costs and your own earnings. A story about how an investment works is incomplete without: \"There's an expense ratio built into the fund, and I earn a commission from the fund house when you invest through me.\" (If you are a fee-based adviser, explain your fee in the same plain way.) Clients who learn this later feel misled; clients who hear it upfront tend to trust you more.\n\nExercise: rewrite one example you often use with clients. Add a clear illustration label, remove any return figure you can't support, and add one line about a possible fall.", "do": ["Describe a possible fall before the client invests", "Disclose expense ratio and your commission upfront"], "dont": ["Show only the best period of a fund's history", "Say or imply that returns are guaranteed or \"safe\""]}], "takeaways": ["Use stories to build understanding, not to push a product.", "Label every example as an illustration and use imaginary characters.", "Connect stories to the client's goals and timelines, then check suitability.", "Explain possible falls before the client invests.", "Never cherry-pick returns or imply guarantees; disclose costs and commission."], "quiz": [["What is the main purpose of a story in a client conversation?", ["To help the client understand a concept and their own situation", "To create excitement about a product's returns so the client feels confident", "To make the client decide quickly", "To show how successful your other clients are"], 0, "Client stories should build understanding, not pressure or excitement about returns."], ["Which phrase best introduces a numerical example?", ["\"This is what you will earn.\"", "\"These are made-up numbers to show the idea, not what any fund will return.\"", "\"Last year's returns will probably repeat, so this is roughly what you can expect.\"", "\"My best client earned exactly this.\""], 1, "It clearly labels the example as an illustration and avoids implying a return."], ["\"If Meera invests ₹5,000 a month for ten years...\" How much of her own money has she put in?", ["₹50,000", "₹5 lakh", "₹6 lakh", "₹60 lakh"], 2, "₹5,000 × 12 months × 10 years = ₹6,00,000, or ₹6 lakh."], ["Why use imaginary characters rather than real clients in stories?", ["Real clients' results are always poor", "Imaginary stories are allowed to show higher returns than real ones", "Regulators ban all examples", "It protects client privacy and signals that it's an illustration"], 3, "Imaginary characters keep real clients' information private and make the illustrative nature clear."], ["A client needs money for a wedding in 18 months. What does a goal-based story help show?", ["That money needed soon usually shouldn't be in something that can fall sharply", "That equity always beats every other option over any period", "That short-term goals don't need planning", "That the client should invest like their neighbour"], 0, "A short time horizon leaves little room to recover from a fall."], ["Which risk illustration is most helpful before a client invests?", ["\"Don't worry, markets always go up.\"", "\"Risk is low here because the fund is very popular and many people have invested in it.\"", "\"Imagine ₹10 lakh showing ₹8 lakh after a bad few months — how would you feel?\"", "\"Risk only matters for big investors.\""], 2, "It shows a realistic fall (20%) and helps you judge whether the product suits the client's risk appetite."], ["A colleague shows clients only a fund's best five-year period. What is the problem?", ["Five years is too long to show", "It is cherry-picking and gives a misleading picture", "Clients prefer one-year figures", "There's no problem, as long as the numbers shown are real and accurate"], 1, "Selective periods, even if accurate, mislead clients about likely outcomes."], ["When should you mention the expense ratio and your commission?", ["Only if the client asks", "After the investment is made", "Never, as it may put the client off and they can read the documents", "Upfront, as part of explaining how the investment works"], 3, "Early disclosure is expected and builds trust; late discovery feels like being misled."], ["A client says, \"My colleague has ₹50 lakh in equity — I should too.\" What's the best response?", ["\"Let's look at your own goals, timelines and comfort with ups and downs first.\"", "\"Yes, you're falling behind your colleague, so let's invest the same amount today.\"", "\"Your colleague is probably lying.\"", "\"Let's invest even more than your colleague.\""], 0, "Suitability depends on the client's own situation, not on comparison with others."], ["Which statement about a past-performance story is correct?", ["\"This fund gave 18% last year, so expect the same.\"", "\"Past performance doesn't indicate future returns; here are the full figures, including weak years.\"", "\"An investor I know made 3x — you can too.\"", "\"This fund is completely safe because it has never once fallen in any year since it was launched, so you can relax.\""], 1, "It uses complete figures with the standard caution, avoiding implied guarantees."]], "scenarios": [{"title": "The \"show me returns\" client", "situation": "Mr. Rao, 45, a government employee in Hyderabad, asks you, \"Just tell me a story of someone who made big money in mutual funds, so I know it works.\" He is saving for his son's engineering fees in four years.", "options": [{"text": "Share the story of a client who tripled his money in five years.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "A cherry-picked success story sets unrealistic expectations and ignores Mr. Rao's short timeline."}, {"text": "\"I'd rather show you an illustration that fits your goal. Imagine a parent with four years to a fee payment: if markets fall a year before, there's little time to recover. So let's talk about how much of this money can handle ups and downs.\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "It redirects from returns to his goal and timeline, labels the example, and opens a suitability discussion."}, {"text": "Show him the fund's official long-term performance, including weak years, with the standard past-performance caution.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Honest and complete, but it still focuses on returns before understanding whether equity suits a four-year goal."}, {"text": "\"Mutual funds always work in the long run — trust me.\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "It implies a guarantee and skips suitability entirely."}]}, {"title": "The nervous first-time investor", "situation": "Priya, 29, an IT professional in Pune, wants to start a monthly SIP for retirement but says, \"What if I lose everything?\" She has never invested in market-linked products.", "options": [{"text": "\"Don't worry, it's completely safe.\"", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Market-linked products are not risk-free; this is a misleading reassurance."}, {"text": "\"Here's an illustration: an imaginary investor's ₹1 lakh might show ₹80,000 after a bad patch. Over a long goal like retirement, there is time for markets to recover, though it isn't guaranteed. How would you feel seeing that drop?\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "An honest, labelled illustration of a fall, linked to her long horizon, plus a question that checks her comfort with risk."}, {"text": "Explain the concept of standard deviation and show her a volatility chart.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "Accurate, but jargon may not answer her real worry; a simple story makes the risk easier to grasp."}, {"text": "Suggest she wait until markets are \"safer\" before starting.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "It implies you can predict safe times and doesn't help her understand or manage risk."}]}, {"title": "The cost question you weren't asked", "situation": "You have explained a balanced fund to Mr. Fernandes, a retired bank officer in Goa, using a clear goal-based illustration. He seems ready to invest and hasn't asked about costs or how you are paid.", "options": [{"text": "Proceed — he didn't ask, and raising it may confuse him.", "rating": "poor", "feedback": "Not disclosing costs and commission falls short of conduct expectations and risks him feeling misled later."}, {"text": "\"Before we go ahead: the fund has an expense ratio built in, and I earn a commission from the fund house when you invest through me. Shall I show you where this is listed?\"", "rating": "best", "feedback": "Clear, upfront disclosure in plain words, with an offer to show the official details."}, {"text": "Hand him the scheme documents and say all the details are in there.", "rating": "good", "feedback": "The information is there, but a brief spoken explanation of costs and your commission is clearer and more transparent."}]}], "cards": [{"kind": "intro", "icon": "briefcase", "title": "Stories that build understanding", "text": "A good story makes \"time horizon\" or \"volatility\" click for a client. A careless one sets false expectations. Learn to tell client stories honestly and responsibly."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "info", "title": "Label every illustration", "text": "Use imaginary characters and say clearly that the numbers are examples.", "bad": "My client invested like this and tripled his money.", "good": "Imagine an investor, say Meera — these are made-up numbers, not what any fund will return."}, {"kind": "tip", "icon": "target", "title": "Talk about what clients control", "text": "Keep numbers simple and correct. Don't attach a return to them.", "example": "₹5,000 a month for 10 years = ₹6 lakh invested. Its value could be more or less."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "clock", "title": "Start with goals and timelines", "text": "Let the story lead into a suitability conversation, not a comparison.", "bad": "Your colleague already has ₹50 lakh in equity!", "good": "Money needed in 18 months usually shouldn't be in something that can fall sharply."}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "shield", "title": "Explain falls before they happen", "text": "Clients who expect a fall are less likely to panic-sell.", "bad": "Don't worry, it's completely safe.", "good": "Imagine ₹10 lakh showing ₹8 lakh after a bad few months. How would you feel?"}, {"kind": "compare", "icon": "chart", "title": "No cherry-picked returns", "text": "Use full official figures, including weak years, with the past-performance caution.", "bad": "Look at this fund's best five years!", "good": "Here are the full figures. Past performance doesn't indicate future returns."}, {"kind": "checklist", "icon": "check", "title": "Before you tell the story", "text": "Tap through each one.", "items": ["Labelled as an illustration", "No real client named", "No return promised or implied", "A possible fall mentioned", "Expense ratio and your commission disclosed"]}, {"kind": "recap", "icon": "trophy", "title": "Honest stories, better decisions", "text": "Keep these in mind with every client:"}]}];
